manifest.json.lock

# Per-run extraction output stays on disk and out of the repository. manifest.json
# beside this file is the durable evidence: it carries each run's model, temperature,
# rules fingerprint, token budget, domain, length class, counts and grounding stats,
# which is where every figure the paper cites came from.
#
# The tradeoff, stated so nobody discovers it later: summarise.py also opens the run
# folders, for the cross-run entity-overlap figure ("found in all five", load_run).
# That part of the summary works on a machine that has the folders and not on a fresh
# clone -- re-run run_variability.py to regenerate them. Everything derived from the
# manifest alone, which is the headline governance and variance result, needs nothing
# but this directory.
#
# Two patterns, because the layout changed. Output now nests by model and temperature
# (runs/<model>/t<temp>/<document>/run_NN) so a study repeated with another model stays
# separable by eye; the old flat layout was <document>/run_NN. The old pattern alone
# did not match the new depth, which would have put 100 extraction folders into a
# commit.
runs/**/run_*/
*/run_*/

# The archived study moved its run folders one level deeper, so they need the same
# treatment: 2.1 MB of per-run extraction output that was never in a commit and must
# not enter one now. The archived manifest IS tracked -- it is the record.
archive-*/**/run_*/
